{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Eviction Law criminal-conviction grounds (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1)","requirement":"A landlord may evict a tenant convicted of or civilly liable for assault, threats against the landlord, theft, drug activity or human trafficking. A three-day Notice to Quit is required, and no suit may be filed more than two years after conviction or release.","key_value":"3-day Notice to Quit; 2-year filing limit","coverage_conditions":"Tenants covered by the State Eviction Law, including a tenant who permits a person with such a conviction to reside at the premises.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Any tenant covered by the State Eviction Law; no building age, unit count or ownership limit stated."},"exemptions":"The drug-related ground does not apply to a tenant who allows a juvenile found delinquent for drug use or possession to reside at the property.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No eviction suit may be brought more than two years after: the juvenile was found to be delinquent; conviction of the person; or after the person’s release from incarceration, whichever is later.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Eviction rules for owner-occupied two- and three-family dwellings (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.2)","requirement":"A tenant in a landlord-occupied two- or three-family dwelling may be evicted only by court order, and only for holding over, nonpayment, disorderly conduct, willful damage, or violating written rules. Notice is three months for at-will and year-to-year tenancies and one month for month-to-month tenancies.","key_value":"3-month notice (at-will/year-to-year); 1-month (month-to-month)","coverage_conditions":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings; eviction requires a court order and proof of a listed cause.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":3,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"Landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings."},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.2","quoted_span":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings can be removed only when a court issues an order for eviction.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Rooming and boarding house eviction requirements","requirement":"Owners of rooming and boarding houses must follow the good-cause and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, unless the Bureau orders otherwise, and must give the County Welfare Board at least three working days' notice before starting an eviction action.","key_value":"3 working days' notice to the County Welfare Board","coverage_conditions":"Rooming and boarding houses — buildings with at least two living units occupied by unrelated persons without private kitchens and bathrooms.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Rooming and boarding houses as defined by the Rooming and Boarding House Act."},"exemptions":"Hotels with more than 85 percent temporary occupancy by people with homes elsewhere, school and college dormitories, buildings housing only college students, and certain residences for the disabled.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)","quoted_span":"owners of rooming and boarding houses to follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, except if otherwise ordered by the Bureau (N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)). There is a further requirement that the owner give at least three (3) working days’ notice to the County Welfare Board before starting the eviction action for any resident  (N.J.A.C. 5:26-3.4(c)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Penalties for Eviction Law violations (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.6)","requirement":"A landlord who evicts a tenant for personal occupancy must occupy the unit at least six months, and one who removes a building from residential use may not use it residentially for five years. Violations bring liability for three times damages plus attorney fees.","key_value":"6 months' owner occupancy; 5-year residential-use bar; treble damages","coverage_conditions":"Landlords who obtain eviction by giving notice of personal occupancy or of permanently boarding up, demolishing or retiring a building from residential use.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"Owner-occupancy evictions and evictions to permanently remove the property from residential use."},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Three times the damages suffered plus attorney fees and costs; for the five-year bar a civil penalty up to $10,000.00 per violation.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.6","quoted_span":"If the landlord allows any residential use of the unit during the five (5) year period from the date the unit became vacant, the landlord, or the former landlord, may be liable to the tenant for three (3) times the damages plus attorney fees and costs.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Reprisal against tenants exercising civil rights (N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10)","requirement":"A landlord may not retaliate by eviction, lease alteration or refusal to renew when a tenant enforces lease or State or local law rights, makes a good faith health or safety complaint, organizes or joins a tenant organization, or refuses a lease change made in response.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"All residential rentals, including mobile homes; owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings are excluded."},"exemptions":"Owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","penalty":"The tenant may sue the landlord for damages in a civil action.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot take reprisal action against a tenant by eviction, substantial alteration of a lease or its terms, or refusal to renew a lease when a tenant exercises certain civil rights (N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Foreclosure eviction notice requirements (N.J.S.A. 2A:50-69 et seq.)","requirement":"Foreclosure alone is not grounds for eviction. Before evicting a tenant because a buyer wants to personally occupy the foreclosed property, the landlord must give a 90-day notice to quit, and a new owner must notify tenants within 10 business days of the sale.","key_value":"90-day notice to quit; 10 business days to notify tenants of a sale","coverage_conditions":"Residential tenants in foreclosed properties, including month-to-month tenants and two- and three-family owner-occupied units, which are not exempt from the 90-day notice.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Residential rental units in properties acquired through foreclosure."},"exemptions":"None stated; month-to-month tenants and two- and three-family owner-occupied units are not exempt from the 90-day notice.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:50-69 et seq.","quoted_span":"Any person acquiring a foreclosed property containing one or more residential rental units must provide notices to the tenants in English and Spanish, within 10 business days after the sale, letting tenants know that ownership has changed hands and that the tenants are not required to move because of the foreclosure.","interaction":"Federal law does not preempt State or local laws that give tenants longer time periods or other additional protections in foreclosure proceedings.","confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act","requirement":"Tenants at least 62 before the conversion recording date, permanently disabled tenants, and certain disabled veterans may be protected from eviction for 40 years if they lived in the building a year before conversion and family income is at most three times the county per-person average or $50,000.","key_value":"40-year eviction protection; income limit 3x county per-person average or $50,000, whichever is greater","coverage_conditions":"Tenants of buildings being converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership who are 62 or older before the conversion recording date, permanently disabled, or certain disabled veterans, and who lived there at least one year before the recording date.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Buildings being converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership; eligibility turns on tenant age, disability, one year of residency and family income."},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.22 to -61.39","quoted_span":"Tenants may be protected from eviction for 40 years if their family income is not more than three (3) times the average per person income in their county or $50,000.00, whichever  is greater (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.28).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Tenant Protection Act of 1992 protected tenancy","requirement":"The Tenant Protection Law of 1992 extends protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties, in buildings converted or being converted, who were not eligible for protected tenancy as senior citizens or disabled persons under the 1981 Act. Hudson County is currently the only qualified county.","key_value":"Hudson County is the only qualified county","coverage_conditions":"Qualified tenants in qualified counties, in buildings converted or being converted to condominium or cooperative, who were not eligible under the Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act of 1981.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Buildings converted or being converted; currently only Hudson County qualifies."},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59","quoted_span":"The Tenant Protection Law of 1992 amendment extends protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible for Protected Tenancy as either Senior Citizens or Disabled Persons under the “Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act of 1981” (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59; N.J.A.C. 5:24-3.1 to -3.4).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Fair Eviction Notice Act warrant notice","requirement":"A warrant of removal must include notice that the tenant has a right to request more time (a stay of execution), and the court will continue the case for up to 10 days after execution of the warrant to hear tenant applications for relief.","key_value":"Notice of right to request a stay of execution; up to 10-day continuance","coverage_conditions":"Tenants in eviction actions in the Special Civil Part of the Superior Court where a warrant of removal is issued.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Eviction actions in which a warrant of removal is issued."},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"Fair Eviction Notice Act","quoted_span":"The Fair Eviction Notice Act requires any warrant for removal to include a notice that the tenant has a right to request more time (called a “stay of execution”).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
